const articleHtml = "<p>A breeding facility has a different disease problem from a single pet household. One infected bird can affect a mate, a clutch, a shared airspace, and the next generation of stock. Screening is useful when it protects those relationships, not when it produces a folder of tests nobody acts on.</p>\n<p>This guide outlines a risk-based testing framework for aviaries, parrot breeders, pigeon breeders, and mixed collections.</p>\n<h2>Why Breeding Facilities Need Disease Screening</h2>\n<p>Breeding brings birds together: pairs, young birds, new stock, and birds returning from shows or sales. Each contact is an opportunity for a pathogen to move.</p>\n<p>Screening supports decisions such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>whether a new bird should enter the breeding area;</li>\n<li>whether a pair should be checked before mating;</li>\n<li>whether a disease problem is isolated or widespread;</li>\n<li>whether a previous positive result has resolved;</li>\n<li>whether a facility's biosecurity is working.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The aim is not to test every bird for everything. It is to catch the risks that would materially affect the breeding program.</p>\n<h2>Common Disease Risks in Breeding Environments</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Management concern</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Viruses</td>\n<td>PBFD/BFDV, avian polyomavirus, pigeon circovirus, PPMV-1</td>\n<td>New stock, young birds, shared airspace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bacteria</td>\n<td><em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>, <em>Salmonella</em> spp., <em>Mycoplasma</em> spp.</td>\n<td>Respiratory disease, hygiene, zoonotic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protozoa</td>\n<td><em>Trichomonas gallinae</em></td>\n<td>Water, oral contact, young birds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fungi</td>\n<td><em>Candida</em> spp., <em>Aspergillus</em> spp.</td>\n<td>Stress, poor ventilation, secondary disease</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The relevant list depends on species and facility history. A parrot breeding room and a racing pigeon loft should not be given the same panel by default.</p>\n<h2>Pre-Breeding Health Screening</h2>\n<p>Before a breeding season, review the birds that will actually be paired.</p>\n<p>A practical pre-breeding check includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a physical examination of both birds;</li>\n<li>a review of previous breeding performance;</li>\n<li>the origin and mixing history of new stock;</li>\n<li>targeted laboratory testing where the risk justifies it;</li>\n<li>a plan for any positive or inconclusive result.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not every pair needs laboratory testing. A closed, stable pair with a good history is different from a newly formed pair of unknown origin.</p>\n<h2>Screening Newly Introduced Breeding Stock</h2>\n<p>New breeding stock carries the highest introduction risk. Separate new birds from the main breeding area while their history and test results are reviewed.</p>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Where did the bird come from?</li>\n<li>Was it mixed with other birds before arrival?</li>\n<li>What was tested, when, and on what sample?</li>\n<li>Is the seller's report linked to this bird?</li>\n<li>Does the species and source justify additional testing?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A negative result from the seller is not a substitute for your own risk assessment.</p>\n<h2>Screening Parent Birds and Breeding Groups</h2>\n<p>Parent birds can be relevant to offspring even when they look healthy. Testing may be considered when there is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a history of poor hatchability or young-bird losses;</li>\n<li>a clinical problem in one member of a pair;</li>\n<li>a new pair formed from birds of unknown status;</li>\n<li>a previous positive result in the facility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Group-level screening can help establish a baseline, but it does not replace individual testing when a specific bird is suspected.</p>\n<h2>Individual Testing vs Group Monitoring</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Best use</th>\n<th>Limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Individual testing</td>\n<td>High-value birds, clinical cases, new stock</td>\n<td>Cost and handling time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group screening</td>\n<td>Baseline and trend monitoring in large groups</td>\n<td>Does not identify every individual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pooled samples</td>\n<td>Efficient laboratory use in defined settings</td>\n<td>Dilutes signal; positive pool needs follow-up</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The right mix depends on the size of the facility and the value of the birds. A small breeder may use individual testing for a few key pairs; a large facility may use representative sampling plus targeted individual follow-up.</p>\n<h2>Environmental Monitoring in Aviaries</h2>\n<p>Environmental samples can provide a signal about contamination pressure, but they are not a direct measure of whether a bird is infected.</p>\n<p>Useful contexts include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>shared water systems;</li>\n<li>high-contact surfaces;</li>\n<li>air and dust in dense housing;</li>\n<li>cleaning and disinfection verification.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Environmental results should be interpreted with bird-level data. A positive surface swab does not identify which bird is shedding, and a negative swab does not prove the environment is sterile.</p>\n<h2>Managing Positive or Inconclusive Results</h2>\n<p>A positive result is a finding, not a complete diagnosis. The response depends on the pathogen, the bird, and the facility.</p>\n<p>A reasonable sequence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>confirm the sample and target;</li>\n<li>separate the affected bird where appropriate;</li>\n<li>discuss confirmation or retesting with a veterinarian;</li>\n<li>review contacts, equipment, and traffic;</li>\n<li>record the action and the outcome.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>An inconclusive result should not be treated as negative. It usually means the laboratory could not produce a reliable answer from that sample.</p>\n<h2>Creating a Long-Term Testing Schedule</h2>\n<p>A schedule should follow management events, not just the calendar:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>new bird arrival;</li>\n<li>pre-breeding;</li>\n<li>young-bird entry;</li>\n<li>show or transport;</li>\n<li>suspected outbreak;</li>\n<li>follow-up after a positive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Review the schedule annually and after any significant disease event. A plan that is never revised becomes ritual.</p>\n<h2>Working With a Molecular Testing Laboratory</h2>\n<p>Ask for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a defined target list;</li>\n<li>sample requirements for each target;</li>\n<li>validation information for multiplex assays;</li>\n<li>controls and contamination management;</li>\n<li>clear reporting of negative, positive, and inconclusive results.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The laboratory should be able to explain what its report does and does not mean.</p>\n<h2>Scenario: Six new lovebirds before breeding season</h2>\n<p>A 40-pair lovebird facility plans to add six unrelated birds before breeding season. The newcomers share an intake room and appear healthy. A pooled sample from the six is Detected for <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>. They have not been paired, but shared feed scoops have moved between intake and a breeding room. This follows the facility's broader <a href=\"/bird-health-screening-biosecurity/\">screening and biosecurity process</a>. The uncertainty is whether one bird, several, or collection contamination produced the signal.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pause movement of the six newcomers and directly exposed pairs. Record room, staff, and equipment contacts, and stop sharing scoops.</li>\n<li>Ask the veterinarian to review the pooled result and arrange individual or smaller-pool follow-up with the appropriate sample type and timing. Our <a href=\"/individual-bird-testing-vs-flock-screening/\">individual versus flock screening guide</a> explains why the pool cannot assign a result to one bird.</li>\n<li>Keep affected breeding starts provisional, separate clean equipment, review hand hygiene, and manage human exposure risk with veterinary advice.</li>\n<li>If individual results are Not Detected, continue observation and ask whether repeat sampling is needed. Do not clear the intake group on one round.</li>\n<li>If Detected or Inconclusive, isolate the relevant birds, retest as advised, and trace contacts. Treatment, culling, or resumed breeding is not decided by the pooled report.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Result</th><th>What changes</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Pooled Detected</td><td>Pause movement and move to individual or smaller-pool follow-up.</td><td>Which bird carried the target or that all six are infected.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Individual Not Detected</td><td>Continue observation; ask whether timing or contacts justify another sample.</td><td>The group is free of infection or safe to pair.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Individual Detected</td><td>Isolate and review breeding, movement, and human exposure with the veterinarian.</td><td>Clinical disease, the exposure source, or that treatment is indicated.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What we would do:</strong> We would stop movement and convert the pooled finding into individual follow-up, because a Detected pool shows that the target was present in the group but cannot identify the source bird. That attribution is needed before breeding pairs and equipment routes are managed. This advice would not apply if birds were clinically ill or a reportable disease were suspected; urgent veterinary and official guidance would take precedence.</p>\n<p>This is a testing-strategy example, not a diagnosis or treatment plan, and a veterinarian must interpret results with clinical findings.</p>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO provides avian molecular testing for selected pathogens. This article is a testing-strategy guide, not a veterinary diagnosis or a guarantee that a facility is free of disease. The current target menu and sample requirements should be confirmed with the laboratory before a breeding-season batch is planned.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Should every breeding bird be tested every year?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A risk-based schedule is more useful. New stock, previous positives, and disease events should drive testing more than a fixed annual rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I test pooled samples from a breeding group?</strong></p>\n<p>Pooling can be practical in defined settings, but it dilutes individual signal. A positive pool needs follow-up; a negative pool does not prove every bird is negative.</p>\n<p><strong>Do I need to test both birds in a pair?</strong></p>\n<p>That depends on the species, history, and risk. A veterinarian can help decide whether one bird, both birds, or a group sample is appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>What if a new breeder has a negative test from the seller?</strong></p>\n<p>A seller's report is useful context, but confirm what was tested, when, and on which sample. It does not replace quarantine or your own risk review.</p>\n<p><strong>Can environmental testing replace bird testing?</strong></p>\n<p>No. Environmental samples show contamination pressure; they do not tell you which bird is infected.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock: Biosecurity. https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>CDC — Psittacosis: About. https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Pet birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds</li>\n<li>WOAH — Newcastle disease. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/newcastle-disease/</li>\n<li>AAVLD — Accreditation requirements. https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n<li>CDC — Bird flu: About. https://www.cdc.gov/bird-flu/about/index.html</li>\n</ol>";
